Training loop
The training loop is the same for every autoencoder in this notebook, so we write it once. It combines four pieces of JAX and its libraries.
JAX transformations (jit, grad, scan) work on pure functions of arrays, while a flax model is a Python object. nnx.split(model, nnx.Param, ...) therefore separates a model into a static description of its structure (graphdef), the trainable parameters (params) and everything else (rest, e.g. the running statistics of batch normalisation), and nnx.merge(graphdef, params, rest) builds the model back. Inside the loss function we merge, run the model, and return the loss. The optimiser comes from optax, where an optimiser is a pair of pure functions: tx.init(params) creates its state (for Adam, adaptive moment estimation, the optimiser used throughout, which keeps running averages of the gradient and of its square, which set the step size of every parameter separately), and tx.update(grads, state, params) returns the parameter updates and the new state, which optax.apply_updates adds to the parameters. jax.value_and_grad turns the loss function into one that returns the loss and its gradient with respect to the first argument, params.
The fourth piece makes the loop fast. At the start of an epoch we draw a random permutation of the \(n\) training images and cut it into \(n_b\) rows of batch_size indices (the last incomplete batch is dropped). lax.scan(step, carry, idx) applies step to each row in turn, carrying the parameters and optimiser state from one minibatch to the next. Combined with jax.jit, the whole epoch, hundreds of gradient steps, is compiled by XLA (Accelerated Linear Algebra, JAX’s compiler) into a single program, with no Python loop over minibatches. The data X are an argument of the compiled function, not a constant baked into it.


Code
def mse_per_image(x, xhat):
    """Reconstruction error e(x) = (1/D) sum_j (x_j - xhat_j)^2, one number per image."""
    return jnp.mean((x - xhat) ** 2, axis=tuple(range(1, x.ndim)))

def train_autoencoder(model, X, epochs, seed, batch_size=256, lr=1e-3, verbose=True):
    """Adam on the pixel-wise MSE; one jit-compiled lax.scan per epoch. Returns the trained model and the loss per epoch."""
    graphdef, params, rest = nnx.split(model, nnx.Param, ...)
    tx = optax.adam(lr)
    opt_state = tx.init(params)
    n_batches = X.shape[0] // batch_size

    def loss_fn(params, rest, xb):
        m = nnx.merge(graphdef, params, rest, copy=True)
        loss = jnp.mean(mse_per_image(xb, m(xb, train=True)))
        _, _, new_rest = nnx.split(m, nnx.Param, ...)           # updated batch statistics (if any)
        return loss, new_rest

    @jax.jit
    def train_epoch(params, rest, opt_state, X, key):
        idx = jax.random.permutation(key, X.shape[0])[: n_batches * batch_size].reshape(n_batches, batch_size)
        def step(carry, batch_idx):
            params, rest, opt_state = carry
            (loss, rest), grads = jax.value_and_grad(loss_fn, has_aux=True)(params, rest, X[batch_idx])
            updates, opt_state = tx.update(grads, opt_state, params)
            return (optax.apply_updates(params, updates), rest, opt_state), loss
        (params, rest, opt_state), losses = jax.lax.scan(step, (params, rest, opt_state), idx)
        return params, rest, opt_state, losses.mean()

    key, history, t0 = jax.random.key(seed), [], time.time()
    for ep in range(epochs):
        key, sub = jax.random.split(key)
        params, rest, opt_state, loss = train_epoch(params, rest, opt_state, X, sub)
        history.append(float(loss))
        if verbose and (ep == 0 or (ep + 1) % 5 == 0):
            print(f"  epoch {ep + 1:3d}: training loss {history[-1]:.5f}   ({time.time() - t0:5.1f} s)")
    nnx.update(model, params, rest)                              # write the trained values back into the model
    return model, np.array(history)


Trained models are evaluated with nnx.jit, the flax version of jax.jit that accepts a module as an argument. Large data sets are processed in chunks of \(1000\) images, which keeps the memory small even for the convolutional networks, and train=False is used everywhere: evaluation never changes the model.


Code
@nnx.jit
def _encode(model, x):
    return model.encode(x, train=False)

@nnx.jit
def _reconstruct(model, x):
    return model(x, train=False)

def in_chunks(fn, model, X, chunk=1000):
    return jnp.concatenate([fn(model, X[i:i + chunk]) for i in range(0, X.shape[0], chunk)])

def encode(model, X):      return np.asarray(in_chunks(_encode, model, X))
def reconstruct(model, X): return np.asarray(in_chunks(_reconstruct, model, X))
def errors(model, X):      return np.asarray(mse_per_image(X, in_chunks(_reconstruct, model, X)))

Summary
A β-VAE minimises \(\mathcal L_\beta=D+\beta R\), the distortion \(D\) (reconstruction error) plus \(\beta\) times the rate \(R\) (the KL divergence of each image’s code from the prior, in nats). For \(\beta=1\) this is minus the evidence lower bound of the VAE. The weight \(\beta\) is minus the slope of the rate–distortion curve at the selected model, and, in the free-energy reading of Lab 024, it plays the role of a temperature that weights the entropy of the code. The optimal code is the posterior tempered by \(1/\beta\). The rate splits exactly into the index-code mutual information, the total correlation of the latent coordinates and the dimension-wise KL. The total correlation is the part that measures dependence between coordinates, and the β-TCVAE penalises it alone.
On MNIST the loss behaves as the theory says. As \(\beta\) grows from 0.5 to 8, the rate falls from 22.9 to 4.5 nats, the distortion rises from 85.2 to 138.5 nats, reconstructions and samples blur, up to five of ten coordinates switch off, and the total correlation falls from 14.50 to 0.26 nats, while traversals still mix style with digit identity, a discrete factor that a continuous Gaussian code cannot isolate. On dSprites, where the factors are known, the mutual information gap measures disentangling. It grows from 0.036 for the VAE to 0.097 for \(\beta=4\) and 0.222 for \(\beta=16\), whose three active coordinates are \(x\), \(y\) and scale, while the β-TCVAE (0.164) isolates the scale at a much lower distortion, and no model learns the orientation. On CelebA a β-VAE with \(\beta=3\) on \(32\times32\) faces finds coordinates for background, illumination, skin and hair colour and hair length, weakly related to the 40 attributes, and the glasses, male and smile directions exist but are faint. The published models, larger and trained longer, isolate pose and smile. On spin snapshots the β-VAE becomes an interpretability tool. In a model trained without labels on Ising snapshots, one coordinate is the magnetisation (\(\rho=0.99\)), and on \(J_1\)–\(J_2\) snapshots the \(\beta=4\) model devotes one coordinate each to \(m\), \(m_x\) and \(m_y\) (\(|\rho|=0.80\), 0.97, 0.98), whereas with \(\beta=1\) the stripe order parameters are mixed. A linear fit by simple functions of the spins then names each coordinate with \(R^2\ge0.98\).
None of this is guaranteed. A rotation of the latent space leaves the data distribution unchanged, so disentangling depends on the assumptions built into the model, the data and the seed (Locatello et al.), and every coordinate found has to be identified afterwards against known quantities. Conditioning, as in the cVAE, is the supervised alternative. It guarantees control of a labelled factor but cannot discover new ones.
References (in the order of first use): I. Higgins et al., ICLR 2017, OpenReview; A. A. Alemi et al., ICML 2018, PMLR 80, 159; M. D. Hoffman, M. J. Johnson, NIPS 2016 Workshop on Advances in Approximate Bayesian Inference, PDF; R. T. Q. Chen, X. Li, R. Grosse, D. Duvenaud, NeurIPS 2018, arXiv:1802.04942; H. Kim, A. Mnih, ICML 2018, PMLR 80, 2649; F. Locatello et al., ICML 2019, PMLR 97, 4114; L. Matthey, I. Higgins, D. Hassabis, A. Lerchner, dSprites (2017), GitHub; Z. Liu, P. Luo, X. Wang, X. Tang, ICCV 2015, doi:10.1109/ICCV.2015.425; A. Radford, L. Metz, S. Chintala, arXiv:1511.06434; L. Wang, Phys. Rev. B 94, 195105 (2016); S. J. Wetzel, Phys. Rev. E 96, 022140 (2017); R. Iten, T. Metger, H. Wilming, L. del Rio, R. Renner, Phys. Rev. Lett. 124, 010508 (2020). For a gentle review of the β-VAE and its capacity-controlled variant see C. P. Burgess et al., Understanding disentangling in β-VAE, arXiv:1804.03599.
