3. Target states
All circuits start from \(|0\dots0\rangle\) on an open chain of \(N\) qubits. We use four families of targets.
The GHZ state \[|\mathrm{GHZ}_N\rangle=\frac{|0\dots0\rangle+|1\dots1\rangle}{\sqrt2}\] is prepared exactly by \(\hat H\) on qubit 0 followed by the chain \(\widehat{\mathrm{CNOT}}_{0\to1},\widehat{\mathrm{CNOT}}_{1\to2},\dots,\widehat{\mathrm{CNOT}}_{N-2\to N-1}\), which has \(N\) gates. Each CNOT can entangle at most one more qubit with the others, so \(N-1\) CNOTs are needed, and at least one single-qubit gate is needed because CNOTs leave \(|0\dots0\rangle\) unchanged. No shorter circuit of our gates exists, and \(N\) is the reference length.
The W state \[|\mathrm{W}_N\rangle=\frac{|10\dots0\rangle+|01\dots0\rangle+\dots+|0\dots01\rangle}{\sqrt N}\] has one excitation shared equally by all qubits. Every amplitude that a product of our gates produces from \(|0\dots0\rangle\) is a sum of terms \(e^{ik\pi/4}/\sqrt2^{\,m}\) with integers \(k\) and \(m\). For \(N=3\) and \(N=6\) the amplitude \(1/\sqrt N\) cannot be written in this form, so the gate set reaches the W state only approximately, and an exact preparation for \(N=3\) would need rotation angles such as \(\arccos(1/\sqrt3)\).
The spin-chain targets are ground states of the Ising chain with open ends \[\hat{\mathcal H}=-J\sum_{i=0}^{N-2}\hat Z_i\hat Z_{i+1}-h\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}\hat X_i-g\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}\hat Z_i ,\] with the coupling \(J\), the transverse field \(h\) and the longitudinal field \(g\). Without the longitudinal field, \(g=0\), and with \(J=1\) this is the transverse-field Ising (TFI) chain, which is integrable. The Jordan–Wigner transformation maps it to free fermions, and in the thermodynamic limit it has a quantum phase transition at \(h=J\) between a ferromagnet (\(h<J\)) and a paramagnet (\(h>J\)) [P. Pfeuty, Ann. Phys. 57, 79 (1970), doi:10.1016/0003-4916(70)90270-8]. We take \(h=0.5\), \(1\) and \(2\). Deep in the ferromagnet the finite-chain ground state is close to the GHZ state, and deep in the paramagnet it is close to the product state \(|+\dots+\rangle\).
A longitudinal field \(g\neq0\) breaks integrability. H. Kim and D. A. Huse chose the antiferromagnetic chain \[\hat{\mathcal H}_{\rm KH}=\sum_{i}\hat Z_i\hat Z_{i+1}+\frac{5+\sqrt5}{8}\sum_{i}\hat X_i+\frac{1+\sqrt5}{4}\sum_{i}\hat Z_i\] as a robustly non-integrable point [Phys. Rev. Lett. 111, 127205 (2013), doi:10.1103/PhysRevLett.111.127205], with \((5+\sqrt5)/8\approx0.905\) and \((1+\sqrt5)/4\approx0.809\). In the convention above this is \(J=-1\), \(h=-0.905\) and \(g=-0.809\). Kim and Huse reduce the longitudinal field on the two end spins, and we use the bulk values with a uniform field, so the target is our ground state of their bulk Hamiltonian. We call it the non-integrable ground state.
The ground states are computed by the Lanczos method. For the transverse-field chain we start the iteration from \(|+\dots+\rangle\). This start vector is symmetric under the flip of all spins, \(\prod_i\hat X_i\), the Lanczos iteration stays in the symmetric sector, and it returns the symmetric ground state even when the two lowest levels of the ferromagnet are nearly degenerate. Every ground state is checked against exact diagonalisation of the dense Hamiltonian in the next cell, which also prints the entanglement entropy defined below and the fidelity of every target with the initial state.
The half-chain entanglement entropy \[S_{N/2}=-\sum_k\mu_k^2\log_2\mu_k^2 ,\] with the Schmidt coefficients \(\mu_k\) of the cut between qubits \(0,\dots,\lfloor N/2\rfloor-1\) and the rest, measures how strongly the two halves are entangled. It vanishes for product states and equals one bit for the GHZ state.


