The entanglement entropy is one measure of how far a target is from the product state \(|0\dots0\rangle\) in which every circuit starts. The results section tests how well it predicts the difficulty of a target for the agent.

9. Fewer gates and fewer CNOTs
The DQN and DDQN agents of the previous sections are now compared with a second family of methods, policy gradient, on the GHZ and W states for the same chain lengths. Before the comparison we sharpen the part of the reward that asks for short circuits, and we apply the same reward to both families.
Three parts of the task already favour short circuits. Every gate costs \(\zeta\), the discount factor \(\gamma<1\) makes a fidelity gain worth more when it comes earlier, and an episode ends as soon as \(F>0.99\), so a circuit that reaches the target early stops paying the gate cost. The gate cost so far is the same for every gate. When a CNOT is more error-prone than a single-qubit gate, a circuit with fewer CNOTs can be the better circuit even when it is longer. We therefore give every gate \(a\) its own cost \(c(a)\), \[r_{t+1}=F_{t+1}-F_t-c(a_t),\qquad c(a)=\begin{cases}\zeta=0.01 & \text{for } \hat H,\ \hat S,\ \hat T,\\ \zeta_{\rm CX}=0.03 & \text{for a CNOT},\end{cases}\] so that a CNOT costs as much as three single-qubit gates. The undiscounted return of an episode with \(n_1\) single-qubit gates and \(n_{\rm CX}\) CNOTs becomes \(F_n-F_0-\zeta n_1-\zeta_{\rm CX}n_{\rm CX}\).
The reference for the learned circuits comes from three sources. The GHZ circuit introduced with the targets has \(N\) gates and \(N-1\) CNOTs, the smallest possible numbers of both, so it is the best circuit for any positive costs. For \(N=2\) and \(3\) the backward induction of the shot-noise study, run with the cost \(c(a)\), gives the largest discounted return \(G_*\) and a circuit that attains it. For every target we also count the gate sequences of the shortest length that reach \(F>0.99\), which tells us how many equivalent shortest circuits an agent can find.


