The printed optima agree with this GHZ circuit for both costs. For the two-qubit W state the CNOT cost changes the best circuit. With the uniform cost the shortest circuit, five gates with two CNOTs, is also the best one. With \(\zeta_{\rm CX}=0.03\) the best circuit has six gates and a single CNOT, so the extra cost of a CNOT outweighs one additional single-qubit gate. For the three-qubit W state no circuit within \(n_{\max}=10\) gates reaches \(F>0.99\), and the best circuit for both costs is the ten-gate circuit with \(F=0.829\) and three CNOTs. For four qubits the search up to nine gates gives \(F=0.562\) for the W state, a lower bound on the best fidelity within \(n_{\max}=12\) gates, since the full search no longer fits in memory.
The counts show that the shortest GHZ circuit is far from unique. There are 2, 4 and 8 shortest sequences for \(N=2\), \(3\) and \(4\), that is \(2^{N-1}\). The Hadamard gate can act on any qubit, and the CNOTs then spread the correlation outwards in both directions along the chain, and the two directions can be interleaved. The two-qubit W state has 8 shortest sequences of five gates, and among the six-gate sequences 58 use a single CNOT.

