8. Bit strings in different bases
A measurement along another axis rotates the qubit so that the chosen axis becomes the \(z\) axis and then measures \(\hat Z\). For the \(\hat X\) basis the rotation is \(\hat H\), and for the \(\hat Y\) basis it is \(\hat H\hat S^\dagger\). The outcome bit \(0\) means the eigenvalue \(+1\) of the measured Pauli operator. On the register each qubit can be measured in its own basis, so a measurement setting is a pair of letters such as \(\hat X\hat X\) or \(\hat Z\hat X\).
The same state produces different records in different settings. We take \(|0\rangle\otimes|+\rangle\), qubit 0 up along \(z\) and qubit 1 along \(+x\). In the setting \(\hat Z\hat Z\) qubit 0 always reads \(0\) and qubit 1 is random. In \(\hat Z\hat X\) both bits are fixed, and in \(\hat X\hat X\) qubit 0 is random and qubit 1 is fixed.


